<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">6 April in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">9 events that happened on 6 April, from 46 BCE to 1930.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-caesar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/46-bce">46 BCE</a> <span class="tih-ago">2,071 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Julius Caesar defeats Metellus Scipio at the Battle of Thapsus</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Near Thapsus, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> defeated the Pompeian forces of Metellus Scipio and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-i">Juba I of Numidia</a>. The victory destroyed the republican cause in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cato-the-younger">Cato the Younger</a>, Scipio and Juba took their own lives.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thapsus">Battle of Thapsus</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cato-the-younger">Cato the Younger</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1320">1320 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">706 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Scottish barons date the Declaration of Arbroath</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>'s nobles sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xxii">Pope John XXII</a> a letter asserting the kingdom's independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and their right to depose any king who submitted to it. It is one of the most celebrated statements of medieval national identity.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1453">1453 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">573 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Mehmed II begins the siege of Constantinople</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> laid siege to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. After 53 days, in which heavy artillery played a major role, the city fell on 29 May, ending the Byzantine Empire and becoming the Ottoman capital.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-constantinople">Fall of Constantinople</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1652">1652 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">374 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Jan van Riebeeck arrives at the Cape of Good Hope</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-van-riebeeck">Jan van Riebeeck</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/table-bay">Table Bay</a> to set up a supply station for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>. The outpost grew into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> and began European settlement in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1782">1782 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">244 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Rama I takes the throne and founds the Chakri dynasty</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The general Chao Phraya Chakri became king of Siam as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a> and soon moved the capital across the river to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>. His Chakri dynasty still reigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> today.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1896">1896 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">130 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">First modern Olympic Games open in Athens</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-i-of-greece">George I of Greece</a> opened the first modern Olympics at the Panathenaic Stadium. Revived by Pierre de Coubertin, the Games drew some 240 athletes from 14 nations.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1909">1909 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">117 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Peary and Henson claim to reach the North Pole</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Robert Peary, Matthew Henson and four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inuit">Inuit</a> companions reported reaching the North Pole. Doubts over Peary's navigation mean historians still debate how close they actually came.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1917">1917 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">109 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">United States declares war on Germany</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Congress approved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">President Wilson</a>'s request for war, provoked by unrestricted submarine warfare and the Zimmermann Telegram. American troops and resources helped tip the balance on the Western Front.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1930">1930 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">96 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Gandhi breaks the salt law at Dandi</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">At the end of his 24-day march to the sea, Mohandas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> picked up a lump of natural salt at Dandi, defying the British monopoly. The gesture launched a mass campaign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-disobedience">civil disobedience</a> across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p></div></li></ol>
